    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.7.1 Ensure /etc/motd is configured"
      info        : "The contents of the /etc/motd file are displayed to users after login and function as a message of the day for authenticated users.

Unix-based systems have typically displayed information about the OS release and patch level upon logging in to the system. This information can be useful to developers who are developing software for a particular OS platform. If mingetty(8) supports the following options, they display operating system information: \\m - machine architecture \\r - operating system release \\s - operating system name \\v - operating system version

The /etc/motd file is displayed after a user authenticates. Displaying operating system and patch-level information - for example the \\m, \\r, \\s, and \\v escape sequences, or the operating system name - provides detailed system information to attackers attempting to target specific exploits of a system. Where an organization uses the message of the day to convey acceptable-use or monitoring notices, that content should follow local site policy. Authorized users can obtain system information directly (for example by running uname -a ) once logged in, so it does not need to be presented in the banner."
      solution    : "Edit any /etc/motd or /etc/motd.d/* file to remove the \\m, \\r, \\s, and \\v escape sequences and any reference to the OS platform, and ensure the remaining contents follow local site policy.

Run the following command to identify files that still contain system information:

# grep -Psi -- \"(\\\\\\v|\\\\\\r|\\\\\\m|\\\\\\s|\\b$(grep '^ID=' /etc/os-release | cut -d= -f2 | sed -e 's/\"//g')\\b)\" /etc/motd /etc/motd.d/* 2>/dev/null

- OR -

If a message of the day is not in use, remove the file:

# rm /etc/motd"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171|3.4.3,800-171r3|03.04.02,800-171r3|03.04.03,800-171r3|03.15.01,800-53|CM-1,800-53|CM-3,800-53|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-1,800-53r5|CM-3,800-53r5|CM-6,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(g),CSF|DE.CM-1,CSF|DE.CM-7,CSF|ID.GV-1,CSF|ID.GV-3,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.IP-3,CSF2.0|DE.CM-01,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|GV.OC-03,CSF2.0|GV.OV-01,CSF2.0|GV.PO-01,CSF2.0|GV.PO-02,CSF2.0|GV.SC-03,CSF2.0|ID.IM-01,CSF2.0|ID.IM-02,CSF2.0|ID.IM-03,CSF2.0|ID.RA-07,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,GDPR|32.4,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.1,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.36,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.37,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ISO-27001-2022|5.2,ISO-27001-2022|5.3,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.1,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.2,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.3,ISO-27001-2022|8.1,ISO-27001-2022|9.3.3,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.1.2,ITSG-33|CM-1,ITSG-33|CM-3,ITSG-33|CM-6,LEVEL|1A,NESA|M1.2.2,NESA|T3.2.3,NESA|T3.3.2,NESA|T7.5.1,NESA|T7.6.1,NESA|T7.6.2,NESA|T7.6.3,NIAv2|CM1,NIAv2|CM1a,NIAv2|CM1b,NIAv2|CM1c,NIAv2|GS8b,NIAv2|VL7a,NIAv2|VL7b,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,QCSC-v1|6.2,QCSC-v1|7.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27892"
      cmd         : "os_id=$(/bin/grep '^ID=' /etc/os-release | /bin/cut -d= -f2 | /bin/sed -e 's/\"//g'); result=$(/bin/grep -Psi -- '(\\\\\\v|\\\\\\r|\\\\\\m|\\\\\\s|\\b'\"${os_id}\"'\\b)' /etc/motd /etc/motd.d/* 2>/dev/null); if [ -z \"$result\" ]; then echo 'PASS'; else echo \"FAIL: $result\"; fi"
      expect      : "^PASS$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : BANNER_CHECK
          description : "banner text"
          file        : "/etc/issue"
          content     : "@BANNER_TEXT@"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "mrsv not included in /etc/issue"
          cmd         : "/bin/grep -Psi \"(\\\\\\v|\\\\\\r|\\\\\\m|\\\\\\s|\\b$(/bin/grep '^ID=' /etc/os-release | /bin/cut -d= -f2 | /bin/sed -e 's/\"//g')\\b)\" /etc/issue | /bin/awk '{print} END {if(NR==0) print \"pass\"}'"
          expect      : "^pass$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.7.2 Ensure /etc/issue is configured"
          info        : "The contents of the /etc/issue file are displayed to users prior to login for local terminals.

Unix-based systems have typically displayed information about the OS release and patch level upon logging in to the system. This information can be useful to developers who are developing software for a particular OS platform. If mingetty(8) supports the following options, they display operating system information: \\m - machine architecture \\r - operating system release \\s - operating system name \\v - operating system version

Warning messages inform users who are attempting to login to the system of their legal status regarding the system and must include the name of the organization that owns the system and any monitoring policies that are in place. Displaying OS and patch level information in login banners also has the side effect of providing detailed system information to attackers attempting to target specific exploits of a system. Authorized users can easily get this information by running the \" uname -a \" command once they have logged in."
          solution    : "Edit the /etc/issue file with the appropriate contents according to your site policy, remove any instances of \\m, \\r, \\s, \\v or references to the OS platform

Example:

# echo \"Authorized users only. All activity may be monitored and reported.\" > /etc/issue"
