    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "nftables.service is not enabled"
          cmd         : "/bin/systemctl is-enabled nftables.service 2>/dev/null || echo 'not-enabled'"
          expect      : "^(disabled|masked|static|indirect|not-found|not-enabled)[\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "nftables.service is not active"
          cmd         : "/bin/systemctl is-active nftables.service 2>/dev/null || echo 'not-active'"
          expect      : "^(inactive|failed|not-active)[\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : RPM_CHECK
          description : "iptables-services package is not installed"
          rpm         : "iptables-services-0.0.0-0"
          operator    : "lte"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : RPM_CHECK
          description : "ufw package is not installed"
          rpm         : "ufw-0.0.0-0"
          operator    : "lte"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "4.1.9 Ensure conflicting firewall utilities are not in use with firewalld"
          info        : "Running more than one host-based firewall manager on a system can produce conflicting or unpredictable rule sets. firewalld manages the Linux kernel's netfilter framework through the NFTables backend; a separately managed nftables ruleset (via nftables.service ), the legacy iptables-services saved rules, or ufw running alongside firewalld can override or conflict with the firewalld-managed rules. Only a single host-based firewall utility should be active on the system.

When multiple firewall management utilities are active, the effective rule set becomes ambiguous and difficult to audit, and one utility can silently override the protective rules configured in another - weakening or negating the host firewall. Ensuring firewalld is the only active firewall manager keeps the enforced policy deterministic and auditable."
          solution    : "For any conflicting firewall manager found in the Audit, migrate any required rules into firewalld first, then disable or remove it.

Stop, disable, and mask a standalone nftables.service :

# systemctl stop nftables.service
# systemctl --now disable nftables.service
# systemctl mask nftables.service

Remove the iptables-services package if installed:

# dnf remove iptables-services

Remove the ufw package if installed:

# dnf remove ufw

Reload firewalld to apply the firewalld-managed ruleset:

# firewall-cmd --reload

Impact:

If a conflicting utility is currently providing required firewall rules, disabling or removing it without first migrating those rules into firewalld could remove needed access controls. Migrate any required rules into firewalld before disabling other firewall managers."
