          description : "5.1.7 Ensure sshd MACs are configured"
          info        : "This variable limits the types of MAC algorithms that SSH can use during communication.

Notes:

 - Some organizations may have stricter requirements for approved MACs.
 - Ensure that MACs used are in compliance with site policy.
 - The only \"strong\" MACs currently FIPS 140 approved are:

 - HMAC-SHA1
 - HMAC-SHA2-256
 - HMAC-SHA2-384
 - HMAC-SHA2-512

MD5-based, RIPEMD-160, 96-bit-truncated, and 64-bit (UMAC-64) MAC algorithms are considered weak and have been shown to increase exploitability in SSH downgrade attacks. Weak algorithms continue to have a great deal of attention as a weak spot that can be exploited with expanded computing power. An attacker that breaks the algorithm could take advantage of a MiTM position to decrypt the SSH tunnel and capture credentials and information."
          solution    : "The MAC algorithms available to the SSH server are governed by the system-wide crypto policy. Disable the weak MACs by configuring the system-wide crypto policy as described in the \"Configure system wide crypto policy\" section.

- IF - CVE-2023-48795 (Terrapin) has not been reviewed and addressed, the Encrypt-then-MAC (EtM) MACs should also be disabled through the system-wide crypto policy.

Once the crypto policy has been updated, reload the SSH daemon:

# systemctl reload-or-restart sshd

Impact:

A too restrictive list of MAC algorithms can lead to a \"no matching MAC found\" error during connection establishment, when a client and the server share no common algorithm. Verify that peers required to connect to this host support at least one of the retained MACs before applying."
