          info        : "/etc/sudoers.d/ is the drop-in directory sudo includes via the #includedir directive in /etc/sudoers. Files placed there become part of the effective sudo policy.

If /etc/sudoers.d/ is writable by a user other than root, that user could add a drop-in file granting unauthorized privilege escalation; if it is world-accessible, the presence and names of its policy files are exposed. Restricting the directory to mode 0750 or more restrictive, owned by root and group root, ensures only root can add, remove, or list drop-in policy files."
          solution    : "Set the owner, group, and permissions on the directory so that only root can add, remove, or list drop-in policy files:

# chown root:root /etc/sudoers.d
# chmod g-w,o-rwx /etc/sudoers.d"
