    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "5.4.1.6 Ensure all users last password change date is in the past"
      info        : "All users should have a password change date in the past.

If a user's recorded password change date is in the future, then they could bypass any set password expiration. The shadow password suite calculates password expiry relative to the last password change date, so an account with a far-future last-change date can permanently bypass password expiration policies: the password never expires regardless of PASS_MAX_DAYS."
      solution    : "Investigate any users with a password change date in the future and correct them. Locking the account, expiring the password, or resetting the password manually may be appropriate.

Examples

Reset last change date to today for a single user:

# chage -d \"$(date +%Y-%m-%d)\" <user>

Force password change on next login (recommended companion):

# chage -d 0 <user>

Impact:

Correcting a user's last password change date to a valid past date may immediately subject the account to the configured PASS_MAX_DAYS expiration. If the corrected date already exceeds the maximum password age, the user may be forced to change the password at next login."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.1,800-171|3.4.2,800-171|3.4.6,800-171|3.4.7,800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.04.01,800-171r3|03.04.02,800-171r3|03.04.06,800-171r3|03.05.12,800-171r3|03.15.01,800-53|CM-1,800-53|CM-2,800-53|CM-6,800-53|CM-7,800-53|IA-5,800-53r5|CM-1,800-53r5|CM-2,800-53r5|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-7,800-53r5|IA-5,CSF|DE.AE-1,CSF|ID.GV-1,CSF|ID.GV-3,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF|PR.DS-7,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|GV.OC-03,CSF2.0|GV.OV-01,CSF2.0|GV.PO-01,CSF2.0|GV.PO-02,CSF2.0|GV.SC-03,CSF2.0|ID.IM-01,CSF2.0|ID.IM-02,CSF2.0|ID.IM-03,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,GDPR|32.4,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.1,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.36,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.37,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|5.2,ISO-27001-2022|5.3,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.1,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.2,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.3,ITSG-33|CM-1,ITSG-33|CM-2,ITSG-33|CM-6,ITSG-33|CM-7,ITSG-33|IA-5,LEVEL|1A,NESA|M1.2.2,NESA|T3.2.5,NESA|T5.2.3,NESA|T7.5.1,NIAv2|GS8b,NIAv2|SS15a,NIAv2|SS16,NIAv2|VL7a,NIAv2|VL7b,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|7.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27892"
      cmd         : "#!/bin/bash
{
 while IFS= read -r l_user; do
 l_change_raw=$(chage --list \"$l_user\" | grep '^Last password change' | cut -d: -f2 | sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//')
 [ \"$l_change_raw\" = \"never\" ] && continue
 l_change=$(date -u -d \"$l_change_raw\" +%s 2>/dev/null) || continue
 if [[ \"$l_change\" -gt \"$(date +%s)\" ]]; then
 echo \"User: \\\"$l_user\\\" last password change was \\\"$l_change_raw\\\"\"
 fi
 done < <(awk -F: '$2~/^\\$.+\\$/{print $1}' /etc/shadow)
} | awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\"; else print \"fail\"}'"
      expect      : "^pass$"
    </custom_item>

    <item>
      description : "5.4.2.1 Ensure root is the only UID 0 account"
      info        : "On a Linux system, the user ID (UID) 0 is the superuser identity: the kernel grants any account assigned UID 0 complete, unrestricted control of the host, regardless of the account's name. The root account holds UID 0 by default.

Limiting UID 0 to the single default root account ensures privileged access is accountable and cannot be silently obtained through a second, easily overlooked superuser identity. Administrative access should be performed through an unprivileged account using an approved escalation mechanism, as described in the recommendation covering restriction of the su command."
      solution    : "- Run the following command to reassign any account other than root that has UID 0 to a new, unused UID (and update ownership of that account's files):

# usermod -u <NEW_UID> <username>

Example:

# usermod -u 1234 bsmith

 - If the root account's UID is not 0, run the following command to reset it:

# usermod -u 0 root"
