    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "5.4.2.5 Ensure root path integrity"
      info        : "The PATH variable for the root user determines the directories the shell searches for executables when root runs a command that is not given as an absolute path. Its integrity depends on the ownership, permissions, and composition of the directories it lists.

Including the current working directory (.) or other writable directory in root 's executable path makes it likely that an attacker can gain superuser access by forcing an administrator operating as root to execute a Trojan horse program."
      solution    : "For each non-compliant item identified by the audit, correct root's PATH as follows.

Edit the shell initialization file that sets root's PATH (for example /root/.bash_profile, /root/.bashrc, or the relevant file under /etc/profile.d/) and remove any current-working-directory (.) entry, any empty entry (::), and any trailing colon (:).

For each directory in root's PATH that is not owned by root, run the following command to set its owner to root:

# chown root <directory>

For each directory in root's PATH that is writable by group or other, run the following command to remove those permissions so the mode is 0755 or more restrictive:

# chmod 0755 <directory>

For each PATH entry that does not exist on the system, either remove it from root's PATH or run the following command to create it with root ownership and an appropriate mode:

# mkdir -p <directory> && chown root <directory> && chmod 0755 <directory>

After making these changes, re-run the audit to confirm root's PATH contains only existing, root-owned directories that are not writable by group or other, with no empty, trailing, or . entries."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.1,800-171|3.4.2,800-171|3.4.6,800-171|3.4.7,800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.04.01,800-171r3|03.04.02,800-171r3|03.04.06,800-171r3|03.05.12,800-171r3|03.15.01,800-53|CM-1,800-53|CM-2,800-53|CM-6,800-53|CM-7,800-53|IA-5,800-53r5|CM-1,800-53r5|CM-2,800-53r5|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-7,800-53r5|IA-5,CSF|DE.AE-1,CSF|ID.GV-1,CSF|ID.GV-3,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF|PR.DS-7,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|GV.OC-03,CSF2.0|GV.OV-01,CSF2.0|GV.PO-01,CSF2.0|GV.PO-02,CSF2.0|GV.SC-03,CSF2.0|ID.IM-01,CSF2.0|ID.IM-02,CSF2.0|ID.IM-03,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,GDPR|32.4,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.1,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.36,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.37,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|5.2,ISO-27001-2022|5.3,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.1,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.2,ISO-27001-2022|7.5.3,ITSG-33|CM-1,ITSG-33|CM-2,ITSG-33|CM-6,ITSG-33|CM-7,ITSG-33|IA-5,LEVEL|1A,NESA|M1.2.2,NESA|T3.2.5,NESA|T5.2.3,NESA|T7.5.1,NIAv2|GS8b,NIAv2|SS15a,NIAv2|SS16,NIAv2|VL7a,NIAv2|VL7b,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|7.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27892"
      cmd         : "#!/bin/bash
{
  l_pmask='0022'
  l_root_path=\"$(su - root -c 'printf \"%s\" \"$PATH\"')\"
  /bin/grep -q -- '::' <<< \"$l_root_path\" && /bin/echo ' - root PATH contains an empty directory (::)'
  /bin/grep -Pq -- ':\h*$' <<< \"$l_root_path\" && /bin/echo ' - root PATH ends with a trailing colon (:)'
  /bin/grep -Pq -- '(^\h*|:)\.(:|\h*$)' <<< \"$l_root_path\" && /bin/echo ' - root PATH contains the current working directory (.)'
  IFS=':' read -ra a_path_loc <<< \"$l_root_path\"
  for l_path in \"${a_path_loc[@]}\"; do
    if [ -d \"$l_path\" ]; then
      while IFS=: read -r l_fmode l_fown; do
        [ \"$l_fown\" != 'root' ] && /bin/echo \" - Directory \\\"$l_path\\\" is owned by \\\"$l_fown\\\" (should be owned by root)\"
        [ $(( l_fmode & l_pmask )) -gt 0 ] && /bin/echo \" - Directory \\\"$l_path\\\" is mode \\\"$l_fmode\\\" (should be 0755 or more restrictive)\"
      done <<< \"$(stat -Lc '%#a:%U' \"$l_path\")\"
    else
      /bin/echo \" - \\\"$l_path\\\" is not an existing directory\"
    fi
  done
} | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\"; else print \"fail\"}'"
      expect      : "^pass$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "5.4.2.6 Ensure root user umask is configured"
      info        : "The user file-creation mode mask ( umask ) determines the permissions applied to newly created files and directories. On Linux the default permissions are 0777 (rwxrwxrwx) for a new directory and 0666 (rw-rw-rw-) for a new file; the umask restricts (masks) these defaults bitwise - bits set in the umask are cleared in the resulting mode.

A umask can be expressed as an octal or a symbolic value:

 - Octal (numeric) value - three or four digits, for example umask 0027 or umask 027 . When four digits are given the leading digit is ignored; the remaining three set the masking for user, group, and other respectively.
 - Symbolic value - a comma-separated list of the permissions to keep for user ( u ), group ( g ), and other ( o ). For example, umask u=rwx,g=rx,o= is equivalent to the octal umask 027, producing a new directory of drwxr-x--- and a new file of rw-r-----.

root's effective umask is the value in force after every source that sets it (the system defaults and root's own shell initialization files) has been applied in order of precedence.

A permissive umask value could result in directories or files with excessive permissions that can be read and/or written to by unauthorized users."
      solution    : "Edit /root/.bash_profile and /root/.bashrc and set root's umask to 0027 or more restrictive by adding or modifying the umask line to read:

umask 027

After making the change, verify that root's effective login umask resolves to 0027 or more restrictive, since a umask set in a system-wide location (for example /etc/profile or a file under /etc/profile.d/) can override the value set in root's shell initialization files.

Note: The recommendation \"Ensure default user umask is configured\" includes guidance to set the default umask."
