    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "5.4.3.2 Ensure default user shell timeout is configured"
      info        : "TMOUT is a shell environment variable that sets an interactive shell's idle timeout, in seconds: after TMOUT seconds with no input, the shell exits. TMOUT=<n> sets the timeout to n seconds (a value of 0 disables it); marking the variable readonly prevents a user from overriding it during the session, and export ing it applies it to the user's shell environment.

Setting a timeout value reduces the window of opportunity for unauthorized user access to another user's shell session that has been left unattended. It also ends the inactive session and releases the resources associated with that session."
      solution    : "Configure TMOUT in a single file. Because best practice marks TMOUT readonly, a second assignment from a later-sourced file produces a \"TMOUT: readonly variable\" error at every login, so it must be set exactly once.

 - Run the following command to locate any existing TMOUT assignments in the shell initialization files:

# grep -PHrs -- '\\bTMOUT\\b' /etc/profile /etc/profile.d/ /etc/bashrc /etc/*bashrc

 -

In each file returned in step one, remove or comment out the TMOUT lines so TMOUT is no longer set in more than one location.

 -

Create a single drop-in file in /etc/profile.d/ that sets, marks readonly, and exports TMOUT in one statement, using a value greater than 0 and no greater than 900 seconds in line with local site policy.

Example:

# printf '%s\\n' \"# Set a 900 second interactive shell timeout\" \"typeset -xr TMOUT=900\" > /etc/profile.d/50-tmout.sh"
      reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171|3.1.10,800-171|3.1.11,800-171r3|03.01.01h.,800-171r3|03.01.10,800-171r3|03.01.11,800-53|AC-2(5),800-53|AC-11,800-53|AC-11(1),800-53|AC-12,800-53r5|AC-12,CN-L3|7.1.2.2(d),CN-L3|7.1.3.2(d),CN-L3|7.1.3.7(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(b),CSCv7|16.11,CSCv8|4.3,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF2.0|DE.CM-01,CSF2.0|DE.CM-03,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(iii),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.7.7,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.1,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.2.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.11.2.8,ITSG-33|AC-2(5),ITSG-33|AC-11,ITSG-33|AC-11(1),ITSG-33|AC-12,LEVEL|1A,NIAv2|AM23c,NIAv2|AM23d,NIAv2|AM28,NIAv2|NS5j,NIAv2|NS49,NIAv2|SS14e,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|8.1.8,PCI-DSSv4.0|8.2.8,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,QCSC-v1|15.2,TBA-FIISB|36.2.1,TBA-FIISB|37.1.4"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27892"
      cmd         : "#!/bin/bash
{
   l_max=900
   a_files=()
   while IFS= read -r l_file; do a_files+=(\"$l_file\"); done < <(grep -Pls -- '^([^#\\n\\r]+)?\\bTMOUT\\b' \\
      /etc/*bashrc /etc/profile /etc/profile.d/*.sh /etc/profile.d/sh.local \\
      /usr/etc/profile.d/*.sh /usr/etc/profile.d/sh.local 2>/dev/null)
   l_set=0
   for l_file in \"${a_files[@]}\"; do
      l_val=\"$(grep -Po -- '^([^#\\n\\r]+)?\\bTMOUT=\\d+\\b' \"$l_file\" | awk -F= '{v=$2} END{if (NR>0) print v}')\"
      [ -z \"$l_val\" ] && continue
      l_set=1
      l_ro=\"$(grep -P -- '^\\h*(typeset\\h\\-xr\\hTMOUT=\\d+|([^#\\n\\r]+)?\\breadonly\\h+TMOUT\\b)' \"$l_file\")\"
      l_exp=\"$(grep -P -- '^\\h*(typeset\\h\\-xr\\hTMOUT=\\d+|([^#\\n\\r]+)?\\bexport\\b([^#\\n\\r]+\\b)?TMOUT\\b)' \"$l_file\")\"
      [ \"$l_val\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$l_val\" -le \"$l_max\" ] || echo \"$l_file: TMOUT=$l_val is out of range (must be >0 and <=$l_max)\"
      [ -n \"$l_ro\" ]  || echo \"$l_file: TMOUT is not marked readonly\"
      [ -n \"$l_exp\" ] || echo \"$l_file: TMOUT is not exported\"
   done
   [ \"$l_set\" -eq 0 ] && echo \"TMOUT is not set in any shell initialization file\"
} | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\" ; else print \"fail\"}'"
      expect      : "^pass$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "5.4.3.3 Ensure default user umask is configured in /etc/login.defs"
      info        : "/etc/login.defs sets system-wide account defaults. Its UMASK value is the umask baseline that pam_umask applies to a login session before any shell-initialization file runs, and that useradd uses when creating a home directory. It governs the umask for sessions that do not source the interactive shell-initialization files.

If the /etc/login.defs UMASK is missing or less restrictive than 0027, sessions that never run the shell-initialization files -- and files created during account setup -- receive permissions readable or writable by other users. A restrictive baseline here ensures a safe default even where shell-level umask settings do not apply."
      solution    : "Edit /etc/login.defs and set the UMASK line to 0027 or more restrictive:

UMASK 027"
