    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "6.1.1.3 Ensure journald is configured to send logs to rsyslog"
      info        : "Data from systemd-journald may be stored in volatile memory or persisted locally on the server. Utilities exist to accept remote export of systemd-journald logs, however, use of the rsyslog service provides a consistent means of log collection and export.

systemd-journald is always present and acts as the initial collector of system log events. Forwarding those events to rsyslog provides a single, consistent path for log processing and export, including reliable delivery to a centralized log server for retention and correlation. When forwarding is not enabled, events collected by journald are not passed to rsyslog for further processing.

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Ensure ForwardToSyslog is set to yes and that no other entry overrides it.

 - Run the following command to locate every file that sets ForwardToSyslog :

# grep -PHrs -- '^\\h*ForwardToSyslog\\h*=' /etc/systemd/journald.conf /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/

 -

In each file returned, remove or comment out the ForwardToSyslog line so the setting is defined in only one place.

 -

Set ForwardToSyslog=yes in a drop-in file in /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/ ending in .conf.

Example:

# [ ! -d /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d ] && mkdir -p /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d
# printf '%s\\n' \"[Journal]\" \"ForwardToSyslog=yes\" >> /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/60-journald.conf

Run the following command to apply the change to the running service:

# systemctl reload-or-restart systemd-journald.service

Impact:

With forwarding enabled, events collected by systemd-journald are duplicated into rsyslog in addition to being retained by journald. Ensure rsyslog log storage and rotation are sized to accommodate the additional log volume this forwarding generates."
