    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "7.1.11 Ensure world writable files and directories are secured"
      info        : "World writable files are the least secure. Data in world-writable files can be modified and compromised by any user on the system. World writable files may also indicate an incorrectly written script or program that could potentially be the cause of a larger compromise to the system's integrity. See the chmod(2) man page for more information.

Setting the sticky bit on world writable directories prevents users from deleting or renaming files in that directory that are not owned by them.

Data in world-writable files can be modified and compromised by any user on the system. World writable files may also indicate an incorrectly written script or program that could potentially be the cause of a larger compromise to the system's integrity.

This feature prevents the ability to delete or rename files in world writable directories (such as /tmp ) that are owned by another user."
      solution    : "- World Writable Files:

 - It is recommended that write access is removed from other with the command ( chmod o-w <filename> ), but always consult relevant vendor documentation to avoid breaking any application dependencies on a given file.

 - World Writable Directories:

 - Set the sticky bit on all world writable directories with the command ( chmod a+t <directory_name> )

Run the following script to:

 - Remove other write permission from any world writable files
 - Add the sticky bit to all world writable directories

#!/usr/bin/env bash

{
   l_smask='01000' a_file=() a_dir=()
   a_path=(-path \"*/containers/storage/*\" -o -path \"*/containerd/*\" -o -path \"*/kubelet/*\" -o -path \"*/docker/*\" -o -path \"/sys/*\" -o -path \"/snap/*\" -o -path \"/boot/efi/*\")
   l_exclude=\"nfs|proc|cifs|smb|vfat|iso9660|efivarfs|selinuxfs|ncpfs\"
   l_exclude2=\"\\/run|\\/tmp|\\/var\\/tmp\"
   while IFS= read -r l_mount; do
      while IFS= read -r -d $'\\0' l_file; do
         l_mode=\"$(stat -Lc '%#a' \"$l_file\" 2>/dev/null)\"
         if [ -f \"$l_file\" ]; then # Remove excess permissions from WW files
            printf '%s\\n' \"\" \" - File: \\\"$l_file\\\" is mode: \\\"$l_mode\\\"\" \\
            \" - removing write permission on \\\"$l_file\\\" from \\\"other\\\"\"
            chmod o-w \"$l_file\"
         fi
         if [ -d \"$l_file\" ]; then # Add sticky bit
            if [ ! $(( $l_mode & $l_smask )) -gt 0 ]; then
               printf '%s\\n' \"\" \" - Directory: \\\"$l_file\\\" is mode: \\\"$l_mode\\\"\" \\
               \" and doesn't have the sticky bit set\" \\
               \" - Adding the sticky bit\"
               chmod a+t \"$l_file\"
            fi
         fi
      done < <(find \"$l_mount\" -mount -xdev \\( \"${a_path[@]}\" \\) \\
      -prune -o \\( -type f -o -type d \\) -perm -0002 -print0 2> /dev/null)
   done < <(findmnt -Dkerno fstype,target | \\
   awk '($1 !~ /^[[:space:]]*('\"$l_exclude\"')/ && $2 !~ /^('\"$l_exclude2\"')(\\/|$)/){print $2}')
}

Impact:

Removing write access from world-writable files, or adding the sticky bit to world-writable directories, can break applications that depend on that access; consult vendor documentation before applying."
      reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171|3.1.4,800-171|3.1.5,800-171|3.8.1,800-171|3.8.2,800-171|3.8.3,800-171|3.13.4,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-171r3|03.01.04,800-171r3|03.01.05a.,800-171r3|03.08.02,800-171r3|03.13.04,800-53|AC-3,800-53|AC-5,800-53|AC-6,800-53|MP-2,800-53|SC-4,800-53r5|SC-4,CN-L3|7.1.3.2(b),CN-L3|7.1.3.2(g),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(d),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.1.10.6(a),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSCv7|14.6,CSCv8|3.3,CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.DS-5,CSF|PR.PT-2,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.7.7,ISO-27001-2022|A.7.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.6.1.2,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,ITSG-33|AC-5,ITSG-33|AC-6,ITSG-33|MP-2,ITSG-33|MP-2a.,ITSG-33|SC-4,ITSG-33|SC-4a.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T1.3.2,NESA|T1.3.3,NESA|T1.4.1,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.2.2,NESA|T5.4.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM1,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|AM23f,NIAv2|SS13c,NIAv2|SS15c,NIAv2|SS29,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|7.1.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|7.2.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|7.2.2,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|5.1,TBA-FIISB|31.1,TBA-FIISB|31.4.2,TBA-FIISB|31.4.3"
